        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Interesting Facts You Probably Didn't Know</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-body dark:text-muted">
          <li>GST was implemented in India at midnight on July 1, 2017, completely overhauling an archaic system of 17 different indirect taxes and 23 cesses in a historic parliamentary session.</li>
          <li>India's GST is one of the very few complex dual-GST models in the world, largely necessitated by its federal structure, with both Central and State governments simultaneously levying tax on a single transaction.</li>
          <li>The GST Network (GSTN) processes billions of complex transactions and over 1.2 crore invoices every single month, representing one of the largest and most sophisticated tax IT systems ever built by any government globally.</li>
          <li>Filing a GSTR-3B is legally mandatory even if your business was shut and had zero transactions in a month. Failure to file a simple "Nil Return" still results in accumulating late fees and can lead to the cancellation of your GSTIN.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Massive GST FAQ Section: Your Most Pressing Questions Answered</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <p class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What happens if I miss the GST filing deadline?</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">Missing a GST deadline triggers automatic late fees (₹50/day or ₹20/day for nil returns) and an 18% annual interest charge on outstanding tax. Crucially, it also blocks your buyers from claiming ITC, which can severely damage business relationships.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <p class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Can I claim ITC on employee gifts or office parties?</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">Generally, no. ITC is strictly blocked under Section 17(5) for personal consumption, food and beverages, and gifts to employees. However, if food is provided due to a statutory obligation (like under the Factories Act), ITC might be available.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <p class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How does GST work for freelancers and consultants?</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">Freelancers must register if their annual turnover exceeds ₹20 lakh (₹10 lakh in special states). Notably, if you provide services to international clients (zero-rated supply), you often need to register regardless of turnover to claim refunds or provide Letter of Undertakings (LUT).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <p class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the difference between GSTR-2A and GSTR-2B?</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">GSTR-2A is a dynamic, ever-changing statement of your supplier's filings. GSTR-2B is a static, frozen statement generated on the 14th of every month. You must base your ITC claims solely on GSTR-2B, not GSTR-2A.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <p class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How do I handle a GST notice for ITC mismatch?</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">Don't panic. First, reconcile your purchase register with GSTR-2B. Identify the supplier who failed to file or filed incorrectly. You must then reply to the notice promptly with supporting invoices, payment proofs, and e-way bills, while following up with your defaulting supplier.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <p class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the GST composition scheme and who can opt for it?</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">The composition scheme is for small businesses (turnover up to ₹1.5 crore, or ₹75 lakh in some states) to reduce compliance. You pay a fixed percentage (1% to 6%) on turnover and file quarterly, but you cannot claim ITC or issue tax invoices.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
            <p class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Are there criminal penalties for GST evasion?</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">Yes, severe evasion involving fake invoices, wrongful ITC claims, or failure to deposit collected tax can lead to prosecution, hefty fines, and imprisonment for up to 5 years under Section 132 of the CGST Act.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

